<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Handan</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Handan is a city in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebei">Hebei</a>, at the foot of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taihang-mountains">Taihang Mountains</a>. It was the capital of the State of Zhao from 386 BCE until its capture by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> in 228 BCE, and a great commercial city of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warring-states-period">Warring States period</a>. Its resistance to the Qin siege of 259–257 BCE, relieved by armies of Wei and Chu, is one of the famous episodes of the era.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/handan.jpg" alt="Handan" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: H2v5o68z · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E6%AD%A6%E7%81%B5%E4%B8%9B%E5%8F%B0_10.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Handan lies in the south-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebei">Hebei</a> province, at the southern end of the province, where it borders <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xingtai">Xingtai</a> to the north and the provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanxi">Shanxi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henan">Henan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shandong">Shandong</a> to the west, south and east. It is one of the oldest cities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, first settled around 6500 BCE by the Cishan culture. Its name has stayed unchanged for over 3,000 years and first appears in the Bamboo Annals. A Tang dictionary explained it as the city at the end of Mount Han, but jade writings found at Houma in 1965 spell the second syllable as the word for red, which suggests that the mountain was named for its reddish-purple colour.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the early Western Zhou the area belonged to the state of Bei, and later to Wey. The Di people conquered it in 661 BCE and held it for over 40 years; Jin defeated them in 588 BCE and absorbed it. In 386 BCE Marquess Jing of Zhao moved the capital to Handan, the state's second capital after Zhongmu, and began large-scale construction. It remained the capital for 158 years until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> conquered Zhao. King Wuling of Zhao made his state one of the most stalwart foes of Qin and pioneered the use of walls to secure new frontiers. Handan fell with the virtual annexation of Zhao by Qin in 228 BCE.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sima-qian">Sima Qian</a>'s Records of the Grand Historian says that the future <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-shi-huang">Qin Shi Huang</a> was born in Handan while his father lived there as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> hostage. By the late Western Han it was remembered as one of the era's great metropolitan centres, with developed commerce, handicrafts and iron working. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-guangwu-of-han">Liu Xiu</a> captured it in his conflict with Wang Lang, it was reduced from a regional capital to a county seat. After the Han, Ye, in present-day Linzhang, overshadowed it as a capital of several dynasties until 580. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiangtangshan-caves">Xiangtangshan Caves</a> nearby are a Buddhist cave-temple complex of the sixth century.</p><p>The opening of the Jinghan railway and Handan station in 1906 gave the county town a new place on a national north-south route, and after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>'s surrender in 1945 Handan was established as a city for the first time.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The prefecture-level city has about 9 million inhabitants, and its municipal government sits in Congtai District. It grew after 1949 through rail links and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> and iron of nearby Fengfeng, with iron and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a>, textiles and other industries. Handan is called the capital of Chinese idioms, with more than 1,500 attributed to it, among them learning to walk in Handan, for imitating others badly. Guangfu Ancient City in Yongnian is associated with Yang Luchan and Yang-style tai chi.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Handan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After a failed siege of <b>Handan</b> in 259–257 BCE, between 230 and 221 BCE King Zheng's generals, among them Wang Jian, conquered the six remaining states.</blockquote><a href="/empire/state-of-qin">State of Qin · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Squeezed by the State of Qin, Wei lost its western lands, though Lord Xinling briefly rallied the states to save <b>Handan</b> in 257 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-wei">State of Wei</a></li><li><blockquote>Zhao moved its capital to <b>Handan</b> in 386 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-zhao">State of Zhao</a></li></ul></section>
